│ patch_description        │ The original program failed because its recursive row/column reordering machinery was     │
│                          │ fundamentally broken relative to the actual objective and API: it returned an empty       │
│                          │ `column_orderings` list, mixed row values across columns via `result_df.loc = ...` with   │
│                          │ `as_completed` (nondeterministic row assignment), mutated/dropped columns, fabricated an  │
│                          │ `original_index` column, and its `find_max_group_value`/`reorder_columns_for_value` used  │
│                          │ `hasattr`/`getattr` on namedtuples that crashed on duplicate/space-containing column      │
│                          │ names (visible in stdout: columns like `review/profileName`,                              │
│                          │ `movieinfo_rottentomatoeslink_movietitle`). The whole approach optimized squared field    │
│                          │ lengths and adjacent-row grouping, which does not match the serial character-Trie reuse   │
│                          │ objective.                                                                                │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The fix replaces the entire editable block with a clean, correct implementation: it       │
│                          │ serializes cells (missing -> "") exactly as the evaluator does, builds a small bounded    │
│                          │ set of candidate global column orderings (original order; frequency-ranked order scoring  │
│                          │ each column by sum(len(v)*count*(count-1)); a length-weighted variant), scores each       │
│                          │ candidate with the exact ideal Trie reuse (sum of adjacent LCPs of the sorted serialized  │
│                          │ rows, using fast C-speed slice comparisons), and returns the best. `col_merge` groups are │
│                          │ kept contiguous. All cells, rows, and columns are preserved; a single global column order │
│                          │ is applied so the returned DataFrame is exactly consistent with `column_orderings` (one   │
│                          │ ordering per row). Row order and values are untouched, guaranteeing validation passes.    │
