│ patch_name               │ full_rewrite_prefix_cache_reorder                                                         │
│ patch_description        │ The previous program failed validation for multiple structural reasons: it returned an    │
│                          │ empty `column_orderings` list (violating the required per-row orderings contract),        │
│                          │ mutated the dataframe shape by adding/dropping an "original_index" column, returned a     │
│                          │ dataframe whose columns were altered by internal bookkeeping, and its recursion contained │
│                          │ broken invariants (e.g., `column_recursion` used positional `i` from `as_completed`,      │
│                          │ losing row identity; `final_result_df.columns` was assigned mismatched names). None of it │
│                          │ aligned with the actual character-Trie prefix-cache objective.                            │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The fix replaces the entire editable block with a clean, correct implementation that: (1) │
│                          │ preserves every cell, row and column exactly; (2) builds 2–3 cheap candidate per-row      │
│                          │ column orderings (global frequency-ranked order with score sum(len(v)*count*(count-1)), a │
│                          │ conditional partition tree using integer factorization and bincount that picks column     │
│                          │ orders per group, and a length-ranked fallback); (3) scores each candidate with the true  │
│                          │ objective — sum of LCPs of lexicographically adjacent serialized strings — using C-speed  │
│                          │ slice equality binary search; (4) returns the best candidate with `column_orderings`      │
│                          │ naming the original column whose value sits at position j of row i, with the dataframe    │
│                          │ keeping original column names positionally. `col_merge` groups are kept adjacent as       │
│                          │ blocks in every ordering. Mixed types/NaN are handled by serializing only for scoring     │
│                          │ while outputting original values.                                                         │
