│ patch_type               │ fix                                                                                       │
│ patch_name               │ trie_prefix_cache_reorder                                                                 │
│ patch_description        │ The old algorithm was broken and misaligned with the objective: it returned a             │
│                          │ global-sorted dataframe with an empty column_orderings list (invalid per-row orderings),  │
│                          │ injected an "original_index" column into the recursion (changing data), used              │
│                          │ Counter(df.stack()) heavily (slow, and mixing all cells as a single value pool), mutated  │
│                          │ dataframes via result_df.update/group views, and asserted wrong shapes when col_merge was │
│                          │ used. Its "group value" heuristics (squared cell length) also do not match the true       │
│                          │ objective: character-level prefix reuse measured by a serial Trie.                        │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ I replaced the editable block with a clean, correct implementation that:                  │
│                          │ 1. Preserves every row/cell exactly; uses object dtype; handles NaN via pd.isna           │
│                          │ consistently.                                                                             │
│                          │ 2. Serializes each cell once (scoring representation str(v), NaN -> "") and reuses those  │
│                          │ strings across candidate constructions.                                                   │
│                          │ 3. Builds up to three candidate per-row column orderings: (a) global column order ranked  │
│                          │ by length-weighted repetition sum(len*count*(count-1)); (b) a bounded conditional         │
│                          │ partition tree on factorized integer codes that picks a leading column per group by       │
│                          │ estimated character savings and orders suffixes independently in each group; (c) a        │
│                          │ deterministic lexicographic-by-serialized-row order as a stable fallback. Optionally a    │
│                          │ third heuristic variant flipping the length/frequency tradeoff.                           │
│                          │ 4. Scores each candidate by the true objective (sorted-string adjacent LCP sum with       │
│                          │ binary-search prefix comparison on identical-prefix strings; identical strings            │
│                          │ short-circuit), picks the best, and returns that candidate's dataframe and valid per-row  │
│                          │ column orderings.                                                                         │
│                          │ 5. Honors col_merge semantics by merging the specified column groups first (merging cell  │
│                          │ text of the merged columns per row), producing one merged column; all other constraints   │
│                          │ (shape, values) preserved. one_way_dep is ignored (it was marked "not used" and does not  │
│                          │ affect data).                                                                             │
│                          │ 6. Time/memory bounded: candidate columns capped, tree depth capped, no per-character     │
│                          │ Python loops on long strings, no pandas value_counts inside loops (uses factorize +       │
│                          │ bincount on wide tables).                                                                 │
