│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ fix                                                                                       │
│ patch_name               │ rewrite_trie_prefix_caching_reorder                                                       │
│ patch_description        │ The previous program was fundamentally broken: its recursive "max group value" logic      │
│                          │ rewrote cells via itertuples attribute access, dropped/renamed columns (adding an         │
│                          │ "original_index" column, mutating column names at depth 0), returned an empty             │
│                          │ column_orderings list, and its assertions/shape checks failed on mixed-type data          │
│                          │ (validation never passed — output only showed the debug banner prints before failing).    │
│                          │ The root cause is that the algorithm did not produce valid per-row column orderings       │
│                          │ consistent with the returned data, and its data manipulation violated the preserve-shape  │
│                          │ contract.                                                                                 │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The fix replaces the entire editable block with a correct, simple, bounded algorithm:     │
│                          │ 1. Preserve data exactly: work on an object-dtype copy, apply col_merge with the parent's │
│                          │ merging_columns (same semantics as before), never add/drop/fabricate cells, return rows   │
│                          │ in original order.                                                                        │
│                          │ 2. Serialize each cell deterministically ("" for missing) for scoring only — stored       │
│                          │ values untouched.                                                                         │
│                          │ 3. Build up to three cheap candidate per-row column orderings: (a) a global order ranked  │
│                          │ by sum(len(v)*count*(count-1)), (b) a global order ranked by count*(count-1), and (c) a   │
│                          │ recursive conditional partition tree that picks, per group of rows, the remaining column  │
│                          │ with the best length-weighted pair-repetition savings (using precomputed integer          │
│                          │ factorization and vectorized bincounts, bounded depth/group size, deterministic ranked    │
│                          │ tail).                                                                                    │
│                          │ 4. Score each candidate with the true serial-Trie reuse objective (sum of adjacent LCPs   │
│                          │ of the sorted serialized rows, computed via C-speed binary search on prefix slices), pick │
│                          │ the best, and emit the DataFrame with each row's cells permuted to its own ordering plus  │
│                          │ the matching per-row column_orderings. All public method signatures are preserved.        │
