├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ fix                                                                                       │
│ patch_name               │ rewrite_valid_prefix_cache_reorder                                                        │
│ patch_description        │ The original program failed validation for several structural reasons: `reorder` returned │
│                          │ an empty list `[]` as `column_orderings` (invalid per-row orderings), the recursive path  │
│                          │ built DataFrames by positional `loc` assignment inside `as_completed` (scrambling rows),  │
│                          │ it dropped/fabricated columns (`original_index`, discarded-column merge-back), changed    │
│                          │ the DataFrame shape when `col_merge` was empty, and its final `sort_values` could reorder │
│                          │ rows inconsistently with the returned (empty) orderings.                                  │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Fix: replace the broken recursive machinery with a correct, bounded construction. The new │
│                          │ implementation:                                                                           │
│                          │ 1. Serializes each row exactly as the evaluator does                                      │
│                          │ (`"".join(row.fillna("").astype(str).values)`), preserving all stored cell values.        │
│                          │ 2. Builds up to three cheap candidate global column orderings: (a) length-weighted pair   │
│                          │ repetition ranking `sum len(v)*c*(c-1)`, (b) pure pair-count ranking, (c) a bounded       │
│                          │ greedy conditional partition tree that groups rows sharing a high-repetition value and    │
│                          │ orders remaining columns inside each group (depth bounded by `col_stop`/`row_stop`, cheap │
│                          │ tail afterwards).                                                                         │
│                          │ 3. Scores each candidate with the true ideal Trie objective — sum of adjacent LCPs over   │
│                          │ the sorted serialized strings — using C-speed binary-search prefix comparison, never      │
│                          │ per-character Python loops.                                                               │
│                          │ 4. Applies the best ordering to produce an output DataFrame with identical shape/values   │
│                          │ and returns valid per-row column orderings consistent with the returned data. `col_merge` │
│                          │ is honored by keeping merged column groups adjacent in the ordering without altering any  │
│                          │ cell values.                                                                              │
