│ patch_type               │ fix                                                                                       │
│ patch_name               │ rewrite_lcp_prefix_reuse                                                                  │
│ patch_description        │ The existing program failed validation for multiple structural reasons: (1) it returned   │
│                          │ an empty list `[]` as `column_orderings`, so the evaluator could not reconstruct per-row  │
│                          │ column orders; (2) it added an "original_index" column, and in the col_merge path         │
│                          │ dropped/merged columns so output shapes were inconsistent; (3) it relied on undefined     │
│                          │ helper methods (`calculate_col_stats`, `merging_columns`) from the parent class and used  │
│                          │ complex thread/recursive logic (`column_recursion`, `recursive_reorder`) that built rows  │
│                          │ via `itertuples` attribute access — which breaks for columns with non-identifier names    │
│                          │ (e.g. 'review/profileName') and mixed types, causing silent row corruption/incorrect      │
│                          │ results; (4) `recursive_reorder` returned a DataFrame whose columns were renamed          │
│                          │ ("original_index" appended to column list), breaking identity; (5) objective was squared  │
│                          │ field lengths/adjacent rows, not serial character-Trie prefix reuse.                      │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The fix replaces the editable block with a clean, bounded algorithm preserving the public │
│                          │ API: serialize cells once (missing -> ""), build a small set of candidate global column   │
│                          │ orders (original order; frequency×length-ranked order; a simple conditional-partition     │
│                          │ order based on grouping by a high-repetition column), score each candidate with the exact │
│                          │ ideal Trie reuse metric (sum of adjacent LCPs of lexicographically sorted serialized      │
│                          │ rows, computed with binary search on prefix slices for speed), pick the best, and return  │
│                          │ the permuted DataFrame (object dtype, same rows/cells) together with valid per-row column │
│                          │ orderings. col_merge columns are placed contiguously (merged semantics preserved without  │
│                          │ altering values). No data is added, dropped, or fabricated.                               │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
