│ patch_description        │ The original program failed validation for multiple correctness reasons: it added a       │
│                          │ fabricated "original_index" column and later dropped it, sorted rows destroying row       │
│                          │ identity, returned an empty column_orderings list (invalid per-row orderings), used heavy │
│                          │ per-cell value grouping that could reorder/duplicate values, and mixed assertion-based    │
│                          │ bookkeeping that broke on real data (e.g., review/profileName datasets) where columns are │
│                          │ near-unique.                                                                              │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The fix replaces the editable algorithm with a bounded candidate-construction approach    │
│                          │ aligned with the real objective (character-Trie prefix reuse):                            │
│                          │ 1. Serialize each cell exactly as the evaluator does ("" for missing, str otherwise) once │
│                          │ per column, reused across constructions.                                                  │
│                          │ 2. Build up to three candidate row-field orderings: (a) a global frequency-ranked order   │
│                          │ using sum(len(v)*count*(count-1)) with deterministic tie-breaks; (b) an alternative       │
│                          │ length/frequency tradeoff using sum(len(v)*(count-1)); (c) a recursive conditional        │
│                          │ partition tree that picks, per group of rows, the remaining column with the best          │
│                          │ length-weighted repetition and partitions rows on its value, yielding per-row orders.     │
│                          │ 3. Score each candidate exactly with the ideal Trie objective: sort the serialized row    │
│                          │ strings and sum adjacent longest-common-prefix lengths, computed with C-speed binary      │
│                          │ search on prefix slices (equal strings short-circuited).                                  │
│                          │ 4. Return the best candidate's DataFrame (object dtype, all original values preserved,    │
│                          │ original row order) together with valid per-row column orderings. Column merge groups are │
│                          │ treated as atomic blocks in their given order, honoring the API constraint.               │
│                          │ No cells are added, removed, or altered; shape and row identity are preserved.            │
