│ patch_type               │ fix                                                                                       │
│ patch_name               │ rewrite_correct_row_prefix_reorder                                                        │
│ patch_description        │ The original program was fundamentally broken: it fabricated an "original_index" column,  │
│                          │ merged/added columns, returned an empty list `[]` instead of valid per-row column         │
│                          │ orderings, performed in-place mutations and row re-sorts that scrambled row identity, and │
│                          │ contained dozens of unused/incorrect recursive paths that asserted or crashed on real     │
│                          │ data (e.g. building a result DataFrame row-by-row via ThreadPoolExecutor with             │
│                          │ `as_completed` writing to arbitrary indices, `Counter(df.stack())` failing on mixed       │
│                          │ types, merges producing shape mismatches). The stdout shows it only ran its prints before │
│                          │ failing downstream.                                                                       │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Fix: replace the editable block with a clean, correct implementation that:                │
│                          │ - Preserves every row, cell, and column exactly (object dtype for mixed types).           │
│                          │ - Returns `(reordered_df, column_orderings)` where `column_orderings` is the exact list   │
│                          │ of column names whose values appear left-to-right in row `i` of `reordered_df`,           │
│                          │ satisfying the per-row ordering contract.                                                 │
│                          │ - Honors `col_merge` by treating each merge group as an atomic block (columns kept        │
│                          │ contiguous in the given internal order).                                                  │
│                          │ - Builds up to three bounded candidate orderings: (1) global frequency-ranked column      │
│                          │ order (score = sum len(v)*count*(count-1)), (2) recursive conditional partition ordering  │
│                          │ that picks a leading field per group by length-weighted repetition (using precomputed     │
│                          │ integer factorization codes, bounded depth/branching), (3) a cheap length-weighted        │
│                          │ variant. Each candidate is scored with the true serial character-Trie objective — sum of  │
│                          │ adjacent LCP lengths over the sorted serialized rows, with binary-search LCP so           │
│                          │ comparisons run in C — and the best is returned. If any step fails, it falls back to a    │
│                          │ trivial identity ordering so all datasets succeed.                                        │
│                          │ - Keeps runtime bounded: factorization and serialization computed once and reused across  │
│                          │ candidates.                                                                               │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
