│ patch_type               │ fix                                                                                       │
│ patch_name               │ trie_prefix_reuse_rewrite                                                                 │
│ patch_description        │ The previous program failed validation for multiple fundamental reasons: (1) it returned  │
│                          │ an empty list `[]` as `column_orderings`, so the evaluator had no valid per-row column    │
│                          │ orderings; (2) the recursive machinery (`reorder_columns_for_value`, `column_recursion`)  │
│                          │ accessed rows via `getattr(row, col)`/`hasattr` on namedtuples, which breaks with         │
│                          │ duplicate/space-containing column names, and mutated cells via `result_df.update(group)`  │
│                          │ and `group.iloc[...] = ...` assignments that can alter stored values; (3)                 │
│                          │ `recursive_reorder` built a result DataFrame without the `original_index` column then     │
│                          │ re-assigned columns including a fabricated `original_index` column, corrupting shape and  │
│                          │ data; (4) the whole approach (grouping by a single "max value", splitting rows in half,   │
│                          │ discarding columns) does not target the actual objective — character-level Trie prefix    │
│                          │ reuse over serialized rows.                                                               │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The fix replaces the broken internals with a correct, bounded construction-selection      │
│                          │ algorithm while preserving the public `reorder` signature, data, and shape: serialize     │
│                          │ cells (missing → ""), compute per-column length-weighted repetition scores, build up to   │
│                          │ three candidate orderings (global frequency-ranked order, an alternate length/frequency   │
│                          │ tradeoff order, and a bounded conditional partition tree producing per-row orders), score │
│                          │ each candidate with the true Trie objective (sum of adjacent LCPs of sorted serialized    │
│                          │ strings, computed with C-speed binary-search prefix comparisons), and return the best.    │
│                          │ All original cells are output unchanged; `col_merge` and `one_way_dep` are honored with   │
│                          │ the existing API semantics.                                                               │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
