│ patch_description        │ The original program failed because it returned an empty `column_orderings` list (invalid │
│                          │ per-row orderings) and its recursive machinery mangled/misaligned DataFrames, sorted      │
│                          │ mixed-type columns (raising TypeError), and dropped the "original_index" bookkeeping      │
│                          │ inconsistently. The fix replaces the whole editable algorithm with a clean,               │
│                          │ data-preserving reordering: serialize each cell (for scoring only, never modifying stored │
│                          │ values), build at most three bounded candidate column orderings — identity, a global      │
│                          │ frequency ordering ranked by len(v)*count(v)*(count(v)-1), and a conditional              │
│                          │ prefix-partition tree — score each candidate with the exact ideal Trie reuse metric (sum  │
│                          │ of adjacent LCP lengths of the sorted serialized rows, using C-speed slice-equality       │
│                          │ binary search instead of per-character Python loops), and return the best candidate       │
│                          │ applied as per-row column orderings. Rows and columns are never added, dropped, altered   │
│                          │ or fabricated; mixed types and NaNs are preserved by using object dtype and string        │
│                          │ conversion only in the scoring path. col_merge is honored via the parent's                │
│                          │ merging_columns as before.                                                                │
