│ patch_type               │ fix                                                                                       │
│ patch_name               │ rewrite_lcp_trie_candidates                                                               │
│ patch_description        │ The original program failed validation for several reasons: (1) it returned an empty list │
│                          │ as `column_orderings`, giving the evaluator no valid per-row column permutations; (2) its │
│                          │ recursive group-value reordering mutated row order/columns inconsistently (e.g. appending │
│                          │ an `original_index` column and renaming columns mid-recursion, producing shapes and label │
│                          │ mismatches); (3) it used expensive per-node pandas `Counter(df.stack())` and thread pools │
│                          │ over `itertuples` rows, causing timeouts; (4) its objective (squared field lengths,       │
│                          │ adjacent grouping) did not match the actual character-Trie reuse metric.                  │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Fix: complete rewrite inside the block. It serializes each cell exactly as the evaluator  │
│                          │ does (NaN→"", str(value)), precomputes per-column factorized codes and length-weighted    │
│                          │ repetition scores once, builds three cheap candidate per-row column orderings (global     │
│                          │ frequency-ranked order, a conditional partition tree that splits rows on the highest      │
│                          │ length-weighted repetition column, and a length-free frequency variant), scores each      │
│                          │ candidate with the true ideal Trie objective (sort serialized row strings, sum adjacent   │
│                          │ LCPs via binary search on prefix slices so comparisons run in C), and returns the best.   │
│                          │ The returned DataFrame keeps original row order, original column labels, and exact        │
│                          │ original cell values (object dtype), with per-row orderings that are permutations of the  │
│                          │ column labels. col_merge is honored via the parent `merging_columns`; all other API       │
│                          │ parameters are accepted and bounds                                                        │
│                          │ (row_stop/col_stop/early_stop/distinct_value_threshold) cap tree depth and branching.     │
