│ patch_type               │ fix                                                                                       │
│ patch_name               │ rewrite_lcp_prefix_sharing_orderings                                                      │
│ patch_description        │ The original program failed validation because it returned an empty list `[]` for         │
│                          │ `column_orderings` (no valid per-row column permutations) and its recursive logic was     │
│                          │ broken (row/column recursion reassembled data via `pd.DataFrame(values.tolist())` losing  │
│                          │ dtypes and row identity, thread-pool futures consumed with `as_completed` wrote rows to   │
│                          │ arbitrary positions, an "original_index" column was fabricated, and the final             │
│                          │ `sort_values` scrambled rows). The output rows therefore did not correspond to the input  │
│                          │ rows and no consistent per-row orderings were supplied.                                   │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The fix replaces the broken recursion with a clean, bounded construction-selection        │
│                          │ algorithm: (1) keep the data frame untouched (values, rows, shape preserved, object       │
│                          │ dtype), (2) build up to three candidate per-row column orderings — original order, a      │
│                          │ global frequency-ranked order scored by sum(len(v)*count*(count-1)), and a conditional    │
│                          │ partition tree that picks, within each row group, the column with the largest             │
│                          │ length-weighted repetition and puts it first for those rows — (3) score each candidate    │
│                          │ with the true serial character-Trie objective (sum of adjacent LCPs of the sorted         │
│                          │ serialized row strings, computed with C-speed slice comparisons), and (4) return the best │
│                          │ candidate's per-row orderings along with the unchanged data. Missing values serialize to  │
│                          │ "" only for scoring; stored cells are never modified. col_merge/one_way_dep parameters    │
│                          │ are accepted and handled with the existing API helpers without altering stored values.    │
