│ patch_name               │ rewrite_trie_lcp_candidates                                                               │
│ patch_description        │ The original program failed validation because it fundamentally broke the required API    │
│                          │ contract: it returned an empty column_orderings list `[]` instead of valid per-row column │
│                          │ orderings, it added an "original_index" column and manipulated/merged/dropped columns     │
│                          │ (altering shape and cells), it used slow per-row `itertuples`+`getattr` heuristics that   │
│                          │ don't match the actual objective (character-Trie prefix reuse computed as sum of adjacent │
│                          │ LCPs over sorted serialized rows), and its assertions/prints were fragile. The fix is a   │
│                          │ complete rewrite that: (1) serializes each cell exactly as the evaluator does             │
│                          │ (`fillna("").astype(str)`), (2) builds up to three cheap candidate row/column orderings — │
│                          │ a global frequency-ranked block order using sum(len(v)*count*(count-1)), a recursive      │
│                          │ conditional partition tree choosing split columns by length-weighted pair repetition      │
│                          │ within each group, and a length-biased alternative — (3) scores each candidate with the   │
│                          │ true objective (sort serialized rows, sum adjacent LCPs via C-speed binary search on      │
│                          │ prefix slices), and (4) returns the best candidate as a DataFrame with identical shape,   │
│                          │ index, and cell values (object dtype, per-row positional permutation) plus consistent     │
│                          │ per-row column_orderings. Merged/dependent columns are kept adjacent as blocks without    │
│                          │ altering any stored values.                                                               │
