│ patch_name               │ serial_trie_ordering_fix                                                                  │
│ patch_description        │ The previous program failed because it returned an empty `column_orderings` list (`return │
│                          │ final_df, []`), violated its own shape assertions (it added an `original_index` helper    │
│                          │ column, merged discarded columns back with a merge that changes row order, and            │
│                          │ mis-handled `col_merge`), used a slow pandas/threaded recursion whose row-reordering      │
│                          │ broke row identity, and never optimized the actual objective (serialized character-Trie   │
│                          │ prefix reuse). The fix replaces the whole editable block with a clean, bounded            │
│                          │ construction: factorize cell strings once; build up to three candidate per-row column     │
│                          │ orderings (a global frequency/length-ranked order, a length-weighted variant, and a       │
│                          │ recursive conditional partition tree over factorized codes); score each candidate exactly │
│                          │ with the sorted-string adjacent-LCP sum (C-speed slice-equality binary search); return    │
│                          │ the best candidate's per-row orderings along with the untouched dataframe (rows and cell  │
│                          │ values preserved, object dtype, NaN cells intact). `col_merge` groups are treated as      │
│                          │ atomic ordering units so merged columns stay contiguous, and `row_stop`/`col_stop` bound  │
│                          │ recursion depth. No columns are added or dropped, no cells altered, and every row keeps   │
│                          │ its identity, so validation of shape, data, and ordering consistency passes.              │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
