│ patch_name               │ conditional_partition_tree_candidate                                                      │
│ patch_description        │ Add a fifth candidate construction: a conditional prefix partition tree. Within each      │
│                          │ group of rows, pick the remaining column with the largest length-weighted pair repetition │
│                          │ sum(len(v)*cnt*(cnt-1)) using precomputed integer factorizations and vectorized bincount, │
│                          │ place it at the front, partition rows by its factorized value code, and recurse on the    │
│                          │ suffix separately per group. This produces per-row column orderings (explicitly allowed   │
│                          │ by the API), which lets rows with different shared leading values get different optimized │
│                          │ orders — something no global ordering can achieve. Guard: only run when col_merge is      │
│                          │ empty, scoring is enabled, total chars <= 30M, and depth is bounded (12 when n_cols>=8    │
│                          │ and n_rows<=200k, else 8), with at most 10 split candidates per node. Score the tree with │
│                          │ the exact sorted-adjacent LCP Trie objective and select it only if it actually beats all  │
│                          │ global orderings. Output applies each row's permutation to that row's values (object      │
│                          │ dtype, all cells preserved) with matching per-row column_orderings. This targets the      │
│                          │ weaker datasets (0.44/0.62 hit rates) where row-conditional structure exists but global   │
│                          │ orders cannot exploit it, while the exact-objective selection prevents regressions.       │
